***************************************
***   DIAGNOSTIC TOOL JOREK2_FOUR   ***
***************************************


Purpose:
--------

  * Perform a 2D Fourier-transformation of the JOREK-results in magnetic coordinates.
    (outputs, e.g., the radial structure of the different temperature modes, T_{m,n})


Compile:
--------

  make jorek2_four


Usage:
------
  
  jorek2_four < jorek-inputfile


Details:
--------

  * The file jorek_restart.rst is read and transformed

  * The output is written to Flux_modes, Temperature_modes, ...

  * Some numerical parameters can be modified by putting a namelist file
    four_params.nml in the current directory. The default parameter values are
    
      &four_params.nml
        nstpts      = 80  ! number of radial points
        deltaphi    = 1.  ! step size in toroidal direction
        nsmallsteps = 3   ! number of small steps between two steps
      /


Source files:
-------------  

  * The main program is diagnostics/jorek2_four.f90

  * The core routines are found in the module diagnostics/mod_fourier.f90


Implementation:
---------------

  * First, field line tracing in the axisymmetric component of the magnetic field is performed
    with the number of start points given by the parameter nstpts.
    (debugging output determine_theta_mag.rr-zz.dat allows to check if deltaphi and nsmallsteps
    need to be adjusted -- this would be the case if the field lines do not close after one
    poloidal turn)
  
  * A fixed number (given by the integer-parameter NEQUIDIST_PTS in mod_fourier.f90) of poloidal
    points is determined for each flux surface; these points are equidistant in the poloidal
    magnetic angle theta_mag
    (debugging output determine_theta_mag.rre-zze.dat respectively determine_theta_mag.rre-zze.2.dat;
    determine_theta_mag.tt-t2.dat gives the correlation between theta_geometric and theta_mag)

  * The physical variables are Fourier-transformed after being interpolated to these points.
    The output is written to the files Flux_modes, Potential_modes, Current_modes, Vorticity_modes,
    Density_modes and Temperature_modes.
